== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## Execution model

FACTS.md:27
- **Walk's overload check reads one bound for two generic overloads whose static parameters share a name** (2026-09-28, climb batch 7R rung J; `compile-ladder/rung-ranges-zz32/probes/rangeoperators/`; row 478). Walk caches one symbolic instantiation per generic function in `symbolicStaticsByPartition` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/evaluator/values/FGenericFunction.java:43-62`, under the team's "TODO This is not quite right"). The key is the function's name and its static parameters, compared by name alone (`GenericFunctionOrMethod.java:44-58`; `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nodes_util/NodeComparator.java:402-404`). Generic constructors share the cache (`GenericConstructor.java:170-178`). So of two overloads `f[\I extends AnyIntegral\](x:I, y:I)` and `f[\I\](x:I, y:I, z:I)` beside `f(x:String, y:String)`, one takes the other's bound. Walk refuses the set with the bounded one declared first and accepts it in the other order; it accepts both orders once the unbounded one's parameter is named `J` (`BoundBoundedFirstSameName.txt` against the other three captures). On the base, `rangeOperators.fss` passed only because the kept `opr #[\I\](r: PartialRange[\I\], size:I)` borrowed `AnyIntegral`, which excludes `String`, from `opr #[\I extends AnyIntegral\](lo:I, ex:I)` (`ro-trace.txt`). After rung J it passes because `Range` excludes `String`. The check between a program's declaration and the library's runs only for a name the program declares at least twice (FACTS, "The library's scalar extension is eight generic declarations"), and only on a run that analyses `FortressLibrary` afresh (rows 98, 342).

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## Landed semantics

FACTS.md:114
- **Under `walk`, the interpreter converts by coercion at its three kinds of type check** (2026-09-26, climb batch 4 rung C, `b628871a2`; `compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/REPORT.md`; for generic declarations climb batch N rung K, `041682188`, `compile-ladder/rung-inference-walk/REPORT.md`): a single function's, constructor's, method's or native's parameter (`NonPrimitive.typecheckParams` and the generic unification path), a generic function's parameter of a declared type among them (row 388, `ProjectFortress/tests/CoercionGenericFnRungC.fss`); an overloaded call when no overload applies without coercion (`OverloadedFunction.bestMatchWithCoercion`, at the team's "TODO add checks for COERCE, right here." of `OverloadedFunction.java:792` at `47437c65f`), generic declarations among the candidates, each instantiated by walk's inference with coercion ("Under `walk`, a generic call's static arguments are inferred with coercion ..." below), the most specific chosen by `Specification/basic/conversions-coercions.tex:477-484` and a call with no most specific refused as "Ambiguous coercion"; and a typed binding or assignment (`LHSEvaluator`, `BaseEnv`, `BuildEnvironments`, `LazilyEvaluatedCell`), where a variable, field or parameter declared with a tuple type converts element by element and an element already of its type is kept (`:708-719`). The conversions are the disambiguator's lifted `coerce_` functions, found at the declared type's top level and chosen on the run-time value, a generic trait's coercion whose static parameters are the trait's own instantiated with the target's static arguments (row 389, `ProjectFortress/tests/CoercionGenericTraitRungC.fss`); the converted arguments of an overloaded call are dispatched as an ordinary call, so the declaration that runs is the most specific for the converted value's run-time type (`Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:73-78`, `:466-468`; `ProjectFortress/tests/CoercionRedispatchRungC.fss`). An overloaded function keeps the coercions per argument types in its cache (`OverloadedFunction.java:760-776`) and re-dispatches every converted call; an overloaded method caches nothing, before the rung or after (`OverloadedMethod.mcache` is declared at `OverloadedMethod.java:28`, read at `:48` and `:56`, and written nowhere). Route A's price, in its general form: an argument whose static type needs a coercion while its run-time type plainly matches another overload dispatches plainly under `walk`, where the compiled run and the specification apply the coercion (`conversions-coercions.tex:525-536`, `:567-570`); gated as two expected failures, `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXCoercionStaticRungC.fss` (the specification's example, 4 under `walk` against 3) and `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXCoercionStaticNarrowRungC.fss` (`g(NarrowOf)` under `walk` against `g(Wide)` compiled, `compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/probes/skeptic/diff-1.txt:255`, `:263`). Not converted, each gated as an expected failure: a declared return type (row 387), a generic trait's coercion that declares its own static parameter (row 513, `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXCoercionOwnStaticRungK.fss`), an overloaded function's tuple-typed parameter or an assignment to a tuple-typed field (395); varargs, keyword and arrow targets are not converted and not measured. Three interpreter tests print differently and nothing converts in any of them: `XXXimmutableTopLevel`, the Java line numbers of a printed stack trace through `LazilyEvaluatedCell.java` and `BaseEnv.java`, and `taskTrace2` and `taskTrace3`, identity hash codes (`Evaluator@hex`); all three are identical once those are normalised (`compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/probes/stay-green-repair.txt`), and they are the brief's stop, for Pavol.

FACTS.md:112
- **Every functional-method name of the library is reserved in every program that imports it**: the disambiguator's `checkForShadowingVar` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/scala_src/disambiguator/ExprDisambiguator.scala:745-763`) rejects a parameter or local named `even` or `numerator` with `Variable even is already declared.` (`explorations/compile-ladder/rung-int-semantics-walk/probes/ShadowEven.txt`), and the specification forbids a parameter to shadow within the reach of a functional method, which is the whole component and every importer (`Specification/basic/declarations.tex:426-432`, `:435-436`, `:454-461`, `:533`). So declaring the spec's `shift(self, k)` on `ZZ` in `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi` alone stops every `walk` run on the library's own `Range` parameters named `shift`: 12 api sites, then 6 in `FortressLibrary.fss`, 30 in `RangeInternals.fss` and a local in `List.fss:311` (`explorations/compile-ladder/rung-int-semantics-walk/probes/shift-collision-layer1.txt` to `layer4.txt`). Rung I (`d6faad28f`) landed it with the renames the specification requires, as ruled in `explorations/compile-ladder/rung-int-semantics-walk/JUDGE.md`: `shift(self, k:AnyIntegral): ZZ` is declared on `trait ZZ` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:544`, the line after `even`; body `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:885-886`, after `RSHIFT` at `:883-884`, bound to `BigNum$LShift`), and the colliding parameters and the `List.fss:311` local are renamed to `amount` (4 sites in `FortressLibrary.fsi`, 6 in `FortressLibrary.fss`, 8 in `RangeInternals.fsi`, 30 in `RangeInternals.fss`, 1 in `List.fss`); the array-origin `.shift(` is unaffected (`explorations/compile-ladder/rung-int-semantics-walk/probes/harness-shift-landed.txt`). No other file in the tree binds `shift` (the only other `shiftLeft(shift: I)` is `CompilerLibrary/FortressLibrary.fsi:2061-2064`, on no source path), so a `walk` program may no longer bind it, as it may not bind `even`; the compiled path, with no prelude declaration, keeps no such reservation.

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## The harness and the gate

